hey guys i'm abby sharp welcome to  Abby's kitchen so I'm about three months  into this mothering game and I am  definitely learning a lot it is an  ongoing struggle and one of the things  that I personally have been finding  really challenging is this whole work  baby balance  I am a working mom  aka a mompreneurs some people like to  call them though I really just like to  see myself as like those two things  happening simultaneously I'm a mom and  I'm a working woman we don't need to  have a specific class of working women  necessarily but I know that's what  people like to call us on the internet  and you know it's it's a struggle it's  so hard I mean and one of the things  that I find so difficult about it is the  overwhelming amount of guilt that I feel  every single day no matter what no  matter what I'm doing I feel guilty when  like my nanny or my husband has to feed  my baby a bottle because I'm shooting a  YouTube video like right now and I feel  guilty when I'm taking my kid for like a  two-hour walk in the middle of a workday  just to get him to sleep for a minute  and I'm feeling like I can't get caught  up on my work and my writing or my my  video content so it's a it's every  single day feels like I'm just never  doing things perfectly or as well as I  want to so it's tough to feel like you  are always just gonna have to be good  enough at each of those worlds they are  always being pulled in both very  opposing directions and so you know not  only is that physically and emotionally  exhausting but I think one of the big  problems around this is just that  feeling of overwhelming i am i doing  enough am i doing enough for my kid  am I being a good enough mother and I  think that there's so much judgment of  working moms especially those who maybe  have helped in raising their kid because  that you know there's the idea that you  know your baby is not being raised by  you you're not forming that attachment  to your child that someone else is maybe  going to be the first to see those first  steps or to teach that first word and  that's like a really heartbreaking  thought that you know  I miss something like that that I might  be absent and and that I'm gonna be  judged hard for that not only by  everybody else but also by myself so  like so many millennial moms I'm on a  lot of Facebook support groups and as  much as these are being great at helping  me figure out what diaper cream to use  sometimes they make me feel like total I  mean there's a lot of moms on here who  are real active advocates of like  attachment parenting and the idea that  you know you know nobody else but mom  can really be the one to take care of  baby especially in those first few  months you want to form that bond and  and I've heard a lot of moms tell  stories about how they could not even  you know leave their child for four six  months or eight months or ten months not  even to get like a pedicure and Here I  am letting you know having child care  since pretty much day one someone to  help me so that I can also  simultaneously run my business and we  kind of do this dance throughout the day  and I'm thinking am I being a bad mom am  i making decisions that I'm going to  regret in the long run am i damaging my  child  am I not forming a bond am i spending  too much time with my baby am i spending  not enough time with my baby what about  my career am i letting that go am i  letting that slide there's so many  questions unanswered and well I guess  I'll just kind of have to negotiate  those over time what I do know is that  you know I'm making the best choice that  I can for me right now and I'm lucky I'm  so grateful that I can make that choice  because I know that so many moms out  there just don't have necessarily that  option to willingly make those those  choices you know some moms just cannot  wait to get back into the workforce but  for whatever reason it just financially  makes more sense for them to be at home  because they can't afford daycare they  can't afford you know a nanny or any  other kind of help and then on the flip  side there are women who  really really want to stay home but they  have to go back to work that their Matt  leave is up and they've got to get back  in there because they need to make make  that financial financially work for  their family and I'm just really really  lucky that I've been able to to make  that choice even though it's not an easy  choice that's never feels easy no day  feels easy it's always a struggle but at  least it's something that I willingly  chose to do so one of the things that I  was not prepared for was how emotionally  and physically exhausting mothering is I  mean I love love love being a mom but  mothering is so so hard and I remember  before I you know gave birth I had this  like idealistic idea of what being a mom  was going to be and I thought you know  stay-at-home moms you got it made it is  it's got to be so fun you get to play  with the baby all day you don't have to  go to work you get to be at home you get  to cook it just seemed like a dream and  the reality is I have so much respect  for stay-at-home moms now because it is  so much work and you know I hear from  other moms who are on that leave now  who-who looking back thought they were  gonna get so much accomplished turn not  leave maybe they're gonna start a new  business they were maybe gonna go back  to school they were gonna live it up and  you get to the end of the day and you  just feel so defeated you feel like  you've done absolutely nothing yet you  have done so much you've been on your  feet all day  you've changed a million dirty diapers  you've been done  you've been puked on you've had to pump  and breastfeed a million times you've  had to rock that kid to sleep over and  over again just for them to fall asleep  for five minutes so that you can maybe  go to the bathroom that's literally what  you've been up to  it is such difficult work and yet such  thankless and selfless work and so you  know I have an amazing amount of respect  for these stay-at-home moms and let me  tell you for those of you who have not  experienced it yourself  because I was one of these people and my  husband was one of these people and  we've all had these thoughts  it is so much work it is not just a walk  in the park where you can just watch  soap operas all day that's not what  motherhood looks like and and and I  think we need to give moms so much more  respect and whether you decide to be a  stay-at-home mom or working mom the one  thing I ask is that we support one  another that we can respect one another  and give each other you know as much  props and support as we possibly can  because trust me we're all just trying  to do the best job we can we're all just  negotiating and giving up something  we're all sacrificing something or  different percentages of something and  that sacrifice doesn't feel good no  matter what side you're on so I think  that if we were to just stop judging one  another and stop guilting one another  we'd all being able to be a lot more  effective and efficient and ultimately  happy as moms and they all say you know  a happy mom means a happy baby and  ultimately isn't that what we all want  for our kids so guys I think the bottom  line is the same way that there is no  one-size-fits-all diet here I think the  same could be said for motherhood I mean  there is no best or right way to be a  mom or a parent we're really just all  trying to survive in this really messy  imperfect world of parenthood and do the  best job that we can and ultimately  we're just trying to keep that little  cat alive right so guys I hope you  enjoyed this video if you did be sure to  give it a thumbs up leave me a comment  below about some of the things that you  have felt really guilty about as a mom I  mean hey whether you're a stay-at-home  mom or a working mom we have all been in  this situation so use this as a  situation to get this off your chest and  of course subscribe to the channel if  you don't already and I'll see you next  time on Abby's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

